1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:02,625
Look at the floor under your feet.

2
00:00:02,692 --> 00:00:05,764
Now imagine your family is buried
right beneath it.

3
00:00:05,831 --> 00:00:09,673
In 2026, scientists reported exactly that,

4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:12,524
on a hill in Turkey called Göbekli Tepe.

5
00:00:12,591 --> 00:00:17,798
Under one house floor: a woman,
a young man, a girl of about twelve.

6
00:00:17,865 --> 00:00:22,799
After thirty years of digging, they're
the first graves ever reported here.

7
00:00:22,866 --> 00:00:25,806
Above them stand people made of stone.

8
00:00:25,873 --> 00:00:27,735
Eighteen feet tall.

9
00:00:27,802 --> 00:00:31,960
The biggest two have arms,
hands and fox-fur belts.

10
00:00:32,027 --> 00:00:33,970
None of them has a face.

11
00:00:34,656 --> 00:00:37,599
So who slept on top of these graves?

12
00:00:37,828 --> 00:00:41,688
The experts who found this hill
said nobody ever lived here.

13
00:00:41,755 --> 00:00:45,550
You're about to find out,
because you're going to be born here.

14
00:00:45,617 --> 00:00:49,570
About ten and a half thousand years ago.
Would you survive it?

15
00:00:50,466 --> 00:00:51,455
Here's the game.

16
00:00:51,522 --> 00:00:54,381
Five moments from your life on this hill.

17
00:00:54,448 --> 00:00:58,271
Before each one, make your guess.
Would you make it through?

18
00:00:58,404 --> 00:01:00,246
And watch the list on the right.

19
00:01:00,313 --> 00:01:03,100
That's what the world was told
about this place.

20
00:01:03,167 --> 00:01:05,743
Let's see how much of it survives too.

21
00:01:05,810 --> 00:01:08,812
You're born in a stone house on the slope.

22
00:01:08,879 --> 00:01:13,802
Rectangular walls and a plaster floor.
The hill rises right behind you.

23
00:01:13,869 --> 00:01:18,655
Above you, on the hilltop,
the giants stand in their stone rings.

24
00:01:18,722 --> 00:01:21,522
They're already
about a thousand years old.

25
00:01:21,589 --> 00:01:24,377
Older to you than the Vikings are to us.

26
00:01:24,444 --> 00:01:28,159
First question.
Do you live to see your first birthday?

27
00:01:28,294 --> 00:01:29,809
Make your guess.

28
00:01:30,844 --> 00:01:33,329
No baby records survive from this hill.

29
00:01:33,396 --> 00:01:36,400
So we borrow from people
who lived the same way.

30
00:01:36,467 --> 00:01:39,703
Look at dozens of societies
without modern medicine.

31
00:01:39,770 --> 00:01:43,165
About one baby in four
died in its first year.

32
00:01:43,232 --> 00:01:46,811
And almost half the children
never reached their teens.

33
00:01:46,878 --> 00:01:51,316
But say you're one of the lucky ones.
You're five, and you have a job.

34
00:01:51,383 --> 00:01:52,969
Catching birds.

35
00:01:53,198 --> 00:01:55,131
In the trash heaps of this hill,

36
00:01:55,198 --> 00:01:58,846
scientists counted at least
84 kinds of birds.

37
00:01:58,913 --> 00:02:02,865
Crows and rooks most of all.
And lots of starlings.

38
00:02:02,932 --> 00:02:05,947
But the small birds
have something strange about them.

39
00:02:06,014 --> 00:02:07,449
Their feet are missing.

40
00:02:07,516 --> 00:02:09,803
The best explanation is glue.

41
00:02:09,870 --> 00:02:12,799
A sticky paste smeared along the branches.

42
00:02:12,866 --> 00:02:16,103
A starling lands, sticks,
and you pull it free.

43
00:02:16,170 --> 00:02:18,176
Its feet stay on the twig.

44
00:02:18,243 --> 00:02:22,135
The researchers think catching
birds was mostly children's work.

45
00:02:22,202 --> 00:02:23,204
Your work.

46
00:02:23,271 --> 00:02:27,054
Verdict: you made it through childhood.
Barely.

47
00:02:27,137 --> 00:02:30,242
And the first
line on that list is already in trouble.

48
00:02:30,309 --> 00:02:32,316
You were born in a house.

49
00:02:32,383 --> 00:02:38,748
In 2015 and 2016, a deep test trench
hit round houses on bare bedrock.

50
00:02:38,815 --> 00:02:44,941
Beside them, hearth after hearth, scraps
from bead making, piles of bone tools.

51
00:02:45,008 --> 00:02:49,140
In 2017,
a trench dug for a drainpipe found more.

52
00:02:49,207 --> 00:02:51,424
Later houses covered the slopes.

53
00:02:51,491 --> 00:02:54,026
People lived here. Lots of them.

54
00:02:54,093 --> 00:02:55,149
Second question.

55
00:02:55,216 --> 00:02:58,588
Can you feed yourself on a bare
limestone hill?

56
00:02:58,726 --> 00:03:00,240
Make your guess.

57
00:03:01,262 --> 00:03:04,810
There are no fields up here.
No sheep, no cows.

58
00:03:04,877 --> 00:03:07,166
Every animal you eat is wild.

59
00:03:07,233 --> 00:03:10,968
Nearly half the animal
bones from this hill come from one animal.

60
00:03:11,035 --> 00:03:12,109
The gazelle.

61
00:03:12,176 --> 00:03:14,340
Wild cattle give the most meat.

62
00:03:14,407 --> 00:03:18,120
Then wild ass, boar, red deer and hare.

63
00:03:18,187 --> 00:03:23,628
From midsummer into autumn, the gazelle
herds come through, and everyone hunts.

64
00:03:23,695 --> 00:03:26,772
But the real surprise
here is closer to bread.

65
00:03:26,839 --> 00:03:32,558
Archaeologists found more than 7,000
tools for grinding and pounding food.

66
00:03:32,625 --> 00:03:36,914
Sites of the same age farther
south rarely reach 500.

67
00:03:36,981 --> 00:03:39,807
The stones
still carry the polish of grain.

68
00:03:39,874 --> 00:03:42,308
Wild wheat and wild barley.

69
00:03:42,375 --> 00:03:46,880
Add wild almonds and pistachios,
and you're eating better than you'd think.

70
00:03:46,947 --> 00:03:49,745
Picture your mother kneeling over a stone.

71
00:03:49,812 --> 00:03:53,012
Back and forth. Thousands of times.

72
00:03:53,079 --> 00:03:56,998
And water? There's no spring on this hill.
No stream.

73
00:03:57,065 --> 00:04:00,639
So the people here cut a pit
straight into the rock.

74
00:04:00,706 --> 00:04:04,191
Twenty-six feet across and nine feet deep.

75
00:04:04,258 --> 00:04:06,689
Channels are cut into the rock nearby.

76
00:04:06,756 --> 00:04:11,043
One was even roofed with stone slabs,
like a drain under a street.

77
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:13,117
It was most likely a cistern.

78
00:04:13,184 --> 00:04:15,961
Every winter, the rain came,
and they caught it.

79
00:04:16,028 --> 00:04:17,835
Then there are the tubs.

80
00:04:17,902 --> 00:04:21,046
Stone basins that hold
up to forty gallons.

81
00:04:21,113 --> 00:04:24,509
A gray crust inside them
may come from soaking grain.

82
00:04:24,576 --> 00:04:26,101
Maybe even beer.

83
00:04:26,168 --> 00:04:28,743
The chemical test wasn't strong
enough to prove it.

84
00:04:28,810 --> 00:04:30,173
So, maybe.

85
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:34,546
Verdict: you eat. Wild food, every bite.

86
00:04:34,632 --> 00:04:37,140
And two more lines come off the list.

87
00:04:37,207 --> 00:04:41,554
Third question. You're grown now.
Could you help raise a giant?

88
00:04:41,685 --> 00:04:43,199
Make your guess.

89
00:04:44,233 --> 00:04:49,564
A big pillar here is eighteen feet
tall and weighs five to ten tons.

90
00:04:49,631 --> 00:04:51,709
As heavy as several cars.

91
00:04:51,776 --> 00:04:54,268
The builders most likely
cut them right here,

92
00:04:54,335 --> 00:04:56,421
from the rock of the hill itself.

93
00:04:56,515 --> 00:04:58,545
One giant never made it out.

94
00:04:58,612 --> 00:05:02,484
It still lies in the rock,
about twenty-three feet long.

95
00:05:02,551 --> 00:05:06,641
Every cut is flint against stone.
Nobody here has metal.

96
00:05:06,708 --> 00:05:08,281
Nobody has the wheel.

97
00:05:08,348 --> 00:05:13,129
Then you carve. A long body.
Two arms bent around the front.

98
00:05:13,196 --> 00:05:17,203
Hands with long fingers
that almost meet above the belt.

99
00:05:17,270 --> 00:05:18,670
A belt with a buckle.

100
00:05:18,737 --> 00:05:23,310
A loincloth of fox pelts,
the hind legs and tails hanging down.

101
00:05:23,377 --> 00:05:26,806
One of them
even holds a fox in the crook of its arm.

102
00:05:26,873 --> 00:05:29,914
And the head is a smooth block. No eyes.

103
00:05:29,981 --> 00:05:31,522
No mouth. Nothing.

104
00:05:31,589 --> 00:05:34,937
Around the giants,
the stones crawl with animals.

105
00:05:35,004 --> 00:05:38,434
Foxes, boars, cranes, scorpions.

106
00:05:38,501 --> 00:05:40,177
Snakes most of all.

107
00:05:40,244 --> 00:05:43,243
More than one carving in four is a snake.

108
00:05:43,310 --> 00:05:47,458
On one pillar, a vulture
spreads its wings over a scorpion.

109
00:05:47,525 --> 00:05:49,827
And a man with no head.

110
00:05:49,894 --> 00:05:53,303
Faces did exist in this world.
Nine miles away,

111
00:05:53,370 --> 00:05:57,410
in the old town of Şanlıurfa,
workers found a stone man.

112
00:05:57,477 --> 00:06:02,029
Life-size, about six feet tall,
with eyes of black stone.

113
00:06:02,096 --> 00:06:03,744
Made around the same time.

114
00:06:03,811 --> 00:06:08,388
It may be the oldest life-size
statue of a person ever found.

115
00:06:08,455 --> 00:06:12,255
So why do the giants on the hill
have no faces?

116
00:06:12,389 --> 00:06:15,957
The man who found them thought
they were something beyond human.

117
00:06:16,024 --> 00:06:18,247
Spirits, or gods.

118
00:06:18,314 --> 00:06:21,585
Honestly, nobody knows. We may never know.

119
00:06:21,652 --> 00:06:24,440
What we do know is how
you'd have met them.

120
00:06:24,507 --> 00:06:26,838
The rings most likely had roofs.

121
00:06:26,905 --> 00:06:31,773
In 2023, diggers found the shapes
of wooden beams left in the rubble.

122
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:35,408
You'd climb down into the dark
through an opening in the roof.

123
00:06:35,475 --> 00:06:37,083
Maybe twenty-five of you.

124
00:06:37,150 --> 00:06:40,358
On a bench in the shadows
waits a stone boar.

125
00:06:40,425 --> 00:06:42,709
It's four and a half feet long.

126
00:06:42,776 --> 00:06:44,637
Painted red around the mouth.

127
00:06:44,704 --> 00:06:47,768
Holding something round
between its front feet.

128
00:06:47,835 --> 00:06:49,993
It might be a human head.

129
00:06:50,635 --> 00:06:54,855
And the bench it
sits on is carved with four human faces.

130
00:06:54,922 --> 00:06:59,487
Verdict: you survive the work.
You helped carve a giant.

131
00:06:59,649 --> 00:07:02,020
But would your building survive?

132
00:07:02,167 --> 00:07:06,762
For decades, the experts said your people
buried these rings themselves.

133
00:07:06,829 --> 00:07:08,044
On purpose.

134
00:07:08,111 --> 00:07:10,726
Fourth question. Your house is on a slope.

135
00:07:10,793 --> 00:07:14,020
Your neighbors live above you.
What could go wrong?

136
00:07:14,152 --> 00:07:15,666
Make your guess.

137
00:07:16,698 --> 00:07:22,343
In 2020, a study rebuilt the hill's
ancient slopes, layer by layer.

138
00:07:22,410 --> 00:07:23,986
Here's what it shows.

139
00:07:24,053 --> 00:07:28,336
The houses sat higher up.
Below them stood the great rings.

140
00:07:28,403 --> 00:07:31,132
And every so often, the slope let go.

141
00:07:31,199 --> 00:07:36,296
Rubble poured down into the rings.
Broken stone, flint, bones.

142
00:07:36,363 --> 00:07:38,578
The trash of a whole village.

143
00:07:38,645 --> 00:07:41,762
Maybe heavy rain set it off.
Maybe an earthquake.

144
00:07:41,829 --> 00:07:44,107
Maybe the weight of all those houses.

145
00:07:44,174 --> 00:07:46,257
And the people fought back.

146
00:07:46,324 --> 00:07:49,984
They built a terrace wall. They repaired.
It slid again.

147
00:07:50,051 --> 00:07:51,482
They repaired again.

148
00:07:51,549 --> 00:07:55,467
That's why the dirt inside the rings
mixes old trash and new.

149
00:07:55,534 --> 00:07:57,188
It came from above.

150
00:07:57,255 --> 00:07:58,858
And here's the twist.

151
00:07:58,925 --> 00:08:02,939
The slide buried the two biggest
giants a third of the way up.

152
00:08:03,015 --> 00:08:04,957
That's what held them upright.

153
00:08:05,024 --> 00:08:08,038
For more than 10,000 years.

154
00:08:08,662 --> 00:08:11,580
Verdict: your building didn't survive.

155
00:08:11,647 --> 00:08:14,162
But it's the reason you can see it today.

156
00:08:14,245 --> 00:08:17,513
Cross off another line.
The hill buried it.

157
00:08:17,580 --> 00:08:22,023
So how did the world
get this hill so wrong for so long?

158
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:26,117
People nearby have their own name for it.
Girê Mirazan.

159
00:08:26,184 --> 00:08:27,971
The Hill of Wishes.

160
00:08:28,038 --> 00:08:32,255
In 1963, a survey team
walked right across it.

161
00:08:32,322 --> 00:08:37,182
They saw pillar tops sticking out of the
field and took them for grave markers.

162
00:08:37,249 --> 00:08:38,825
In 1994,

163
00:08:38,892 --> 00:08:43,685
German archaeologist Klaus Schmidt
climbed the hill and saw what they were.

164
00:08:43,752 --> 00:08:48,683
Near his rings he found no houses,
no water and no farm animals.

165
00:08:48,750 --> 00:08:51,045
So he wrote a famous line.

166
00:08:51,112 --> 00:08:53,854
First came the temple. Then the city.

167
00:08:53,921 --> 00:08:58,261
The world loved it.
UNESCO listed the hill in 2018.

168
00:08:58,328 --> 00:09:02,903
In 2019 alone,
more than 400,000 people came.

169
00:09:02,970 --> 00:09:06,729
Schmidt died in 2014.
His team kept digging,

170
00:09:06,796 --> 00:09:09,480
and they are the ones
who took the story back.

171
00:09:09,547 --> 00:09:12,194
In 2020, his successor wrote it plainly.

172
00:09:12,261 --> 00:09:15,314
The temple idea was delivered
and cultivated

173
00:09:15,381 --> 00:09:17,816
by the archaeologists themselves.

174
00:09:17,883 --> 00:09:19,030
That's rare.

175
00:09:19,097 --> 00:09:22,175
And it's exactly how
science is supposed to work.

176
00:09:22,242 --> 00:09:26,705
You may have heard wilder stories.
A lost advanced civilization.

177
00:09:26,772 --> 00:09:29,059
A comet carved in stone.

178
00:09:29,126 --> 00:09:31,223
Archaeologists answered those too.

179
00:09:31,290 --> 00:09:36,231
The comet date is 700 to a thousand
years too early for its building.

180
00:09:36,298 --> 00:09:39,470
And remember,
the rings most likely had roofs.

181
00:09:39,537 --> 00:09:42,047
It's hard to read the stars
through a ceiling.

182
00:09:42,114 --> 00:09:47,086
Even the stones get heavier online.
You'll read forty to sixty tons.

183
00:09:47,153 --> 00:09:49,493
The standing giants weigh five to ten.

184
00:09:49,560 --> 00:09:51,268
The truth is stranger.

185
00:09:51,335 --> 00:09:55,411
Hunters with no farms and no
metal built these giants.

186
00:09:55,478 --> 00:09:57,256
And then they stayed.

187
00:09:57,323 --> 00:10:00,542
They rebuilt the same rooms
for more than 500.

188
00:10:00,609 --> 00:10:01,624
years.

189
00:10:01,691 --> 00:10:05,126
Last question.
When you die, where do you go?

190
00:10:05,253 --> 00:10:06,767
Make your guess.

191
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:11,082
For years, this hill had a dark puzzle.

192
00:10:11,149 --> 00:10:15,868
Pieces of human bone kept turning
up in the rubble, but never a grave.

193
00:10:15,935 --> 00:10:19,565
By 2017, there were 691.

194
00:10:19,632 --> 00:10:22,491
408 came from skulls.

195
00:10:22,558 --> 00:10:25,179
And three of those skulls had been carved.

196
00:10:25,678 --> 00:10:29,526
Deep grooves ran
along the top of the head, front to back.

197
00:10:29,593 --> 00:10:32,308
Stroke after stroke, with flint.

198
00:10:32,375 --> 00:10:36,622
One skull also had a hole drilled
through it, probably for a cord.

199
00:10:36,689 --> 00:10:38,753
It lay in red ochre.

200
00:10:38,820 --> 00:10:42,049
Someone hung a human head
where people could see it.

201
00:10:42,688 --> 00:10:44,614
Someone they loved? An enemy?

202
00:10:44,681 --> 00:10:47,269
The scientists who
studied it don't choose.

203
00:10:47,336 --> 00:10:48,481
Neither will we.

204
00:10:48,548 --> 00:10:52,618
But one question stayed open.
Where were all the graves?

205
00:10:52,831 --> 00:10:55,203
Remember the house
floor from the beginning?

206
00:10:55,330 --> 00:10:59,979
Someone cut a pit into that plaster
and laid a body in it, whole.

207
00:11:00,046 --> 00:11:04,169
Later they opened it, pushed the bones
aside and laid in the next.

208
00:11:04,236 --> 00:11:05,521
Then the next.

209
00:11:05,588 --> 00:11:08,660
The skulls were gathered
together in one corner.

210
00:11:08,727 --> 00:11:12,194
A woman past thirty-five.
A man in his twenties.

211
00:11:12,261 --> 00:11:14,907
A girl between eleven and fourteen.

212
00:11:14,974 --> 00:11:17,177
Not far away, a second grave.

213
00:11:17,244 --> 00:11:21,904
A young woman lying on her side
on a bed of stones, facing east.

214
00:11:21,971 --> 00:11:26,760
Their bones carry exactly the same damage
as all those scattered pieces.

215
00:11:26,827 --> 00:11:31,018
Breaks. Crusts of lime.
The bite marks of rodents.

216
00:11:31,085 --> 00:11:34,686
So many of the mystery bones
began in graves like these.

217
00:11:34,753 --> 00:11:37,191
Under the floors of houses higher up.

218
00:11:37,258 --> 00:11:40,558
Then the slope carried
them down into the rings.

219
00:11:40,711 --> 00:11:43,812
So where do you go when you die?
Under the floor.

220
00:11:43,879 --> 00:11:45,751
Under your family's feet.

221
00:11:46,765 --> 00:11:49,822
Verdict: you don't survive. Nobody does.

222
00:11:49,889 --> 00:11:51,403
But you stay home.

223
00:11:51,482 --> 00:11:56,060
And the last line comes off the list.
There were graves all along.

224
00:11:56,127 --> 00:11:58,201
So, your final score.

225
00:11:58,268 --> 00:12:02,345
Born: you made it, barely.
Fed: you made it.

226
00:12:02,412 --> 00:12:04,843
The giants: you made them.

227
00:12:04,910 --> 00:12:09,198
Your home: the hill took it.
And you stayed, under the floor.

228
00:12:09,265 --> 00:12:12,334
How many did you guess right?
Tell us in the comments.

229
00:12:12,401 --> 00:12:14,973
And the hill is far from finished.

230
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:20,469
Scans suggest at least twenty
rings in all, with more than 200 giants.

231
00:12:20,536 --> 00:12:22,805
Most of them nobody has seen.

232
00:12:22,872 --> 00:12:26,430
Still waiting in the dark.
Still with no faces.

233
00:12:26,971 --> 00:12:28,268
And it isn't alone.

234
00:12:28,335 --> 00:12:31,481
A dozen sites like it
lie in the hills around it.

235
00:12:31,548 --> 00:12:37,236
At one, Karahan Tepe, a room cut
into the bedrock is full of stone pillars.

236
00:12:37,303 --> 00:12:39,943
A human head pushes out of its wall.

237
00:12:40,010 --> 00:12:43,618
And on the mountain next door,
a wild grass is growing.

238
00:12:43,685 --> 00:12:45,548
Wild einkorn wheat.

239
00:12:45,615 --> 00:12:50,045
One day your great-great-grandchildren
will plant it on purpose.

240
00:12:50,112 --> 00:12:55,352
Its DNA points to that mountain as
one of the first places wheat was farmed.

241
00:12:55,419 --> 00:12:57,715
That's where our journey goes next.

242
00:12:57,782 --> 00:13:01,569
But first, look down at the floor
under your feet one more time.

